            <ArticleTitle>Oral contraceptive use and risk of endometriosis. Italian Endometriosis Study Group.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>695-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyse the association between use of oral contraception and risk of pelvic endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">We compared use of oral contraception in women with and without endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Eligible for the study were women with primary or secondary infertility (n = 393) or chronic pelvic pain (n = 424), requiring laparoscopy, consecutively observed between September 1995 and January 1996 in 15 obstetrics and gynaecology departments in Italy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Out of the 817 women included in the study, 345 had a diagnosis of endometriosis; 164 (47.5%) women with endometriosis and 139 (29.4%) without the disease reported ever using oral contraception. In comparison with never users the estimated odds ratios (OR) of endometriosis were 1.8 (95% CI 1.0-3.3) in current users and 1.6 (95% CI 1.1-2.4) in ex-users. No clear relation emerged between duration of oral contraceptive use and risk of endometriosis. In comparison with never users, the OR was 1.8 (95% CI 1.1-3.0) for women reporting their last use of oral contraception &lt; 5 years before interview and 1.5 (95% CI 0.9-2.5) for those reporting their last use &gt; or = 5 years before interview.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The study suggests that oral contraception is associated with an increased risk of endometriosis but this finding is based on a selected population and cannot generalised to all women with endometriosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
